//? Title: Spatial Gaussian Hierarchies and a Miniature NVAE
//? Subtitle: Learned conditional priors, residual posteriors and explicit batch-normalization state
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A hierarchical VAE samples a global Gaussian group, then conditions a spatial latent group on
both the image and the sampled parent. This report runs a two-group spatial hierarchy and a
miniature NVAE adaptation with additive posterior corrections, one depthwise/BN/Swish/SE
residual cell and penalties on two rank-one prior kernels. Both preserve complete visible
three-epoch fitting, separate moving statistics, explicit noise and validation-only checkpoint
selection. On294 known-factor binary images, each receives21 Adam updates and selects epoch3.
Heldout negative ELBO is239.568/297.564nats/image; recursive greedy-mean MSE0.10409/0.10624 is
worse than fitting mean0.04855. The architectures differ, so this is not a controlled quality
comparison. Unfiltered reconstructions and ancestral samples retain the short runs' limitations.
The miniature model does not reproduce full NVAE's depth, scale or all-kernel training recipe.
)
\toc

> Two stochastic groups, with both samples reaching the decoder

Let z2 be a global two-coordinate latent and z1 a7×7 one-channel spatial latent. The
model factorizes as p(z2)p(z1|z2)p(x|z1,z2). Two3×3 Swish convolutions and2×2 average pools
produce a7×7×4 image feature map. Dense heads predict q(z2|x); supplied top noise samples z2.
A dense projection and cell turn z2 into a7×7×4 top feature, giving learned Gaussian
p(z1|z2). A fusion of image and top features predicts the lower posterior q(z1|x,z2).
Only **sampled latent groups** reach the decoder: the image feature is used for inference,
not a deterministic image-to-decoder bypass.

[Vahdat and Kautz's NVAE](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.03898) combines residual Gaussian
parameterization with a carefully designed hierarchical architecture and spectral regularization.
This small teaching adaptation uses just two groups, one residual cell and two penalized
prior kernels. The ordinary hierarchy uses direct lower posterior heads and a simple Swish
cell. These are different networks, not a matched ablation. Related hierarchical inference is
described by [Sonderby et al.'s Ladder VAE](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.02282); the present
posterior uses direct/additive heads rather than precision-weighted evidence/prior fusion.

