\caption(The lower posterior depends on the sampled top code, while the generative decoder uses both latent groups. The dashed prior-to-posterior edge is the NVAE residual correction only.)
\alttext(An image encoder supplies top Gaussian heads and lower inference features. Top samples produce a learned lower prior and a top feature; the lower posterior samples its spatial code. Only the two sampled latent paths reach decoding.)

> A conditional Gaussian KL by hand

For posterior N(mu,diag(exp(l))) and prior N(pm,diag(exp(pl))),

$$
K_1=\frac12\sum_d\left[(pl_d-l_d)+e^{l_d-pl_d}
+(mu_d-pm_d)^2e^{-pl_d}-1\right].
$$
\alttext(The conditional diagonal-Gaussian KL sums log-variance differences, variance ratios and prior-precision-weighted mean differences.)

The direct hierarchy sets(mu,l)=(hm,hl). NVAE instead sets(mu,l)=(pm+hm,pl+hl).
Zero residual heads therefore give the prior and exact zero KL. This is additive mean/log-
variance correction, not Gaussian precision fusion. All prior/head paths remain differentiable.
For one coordinate with prior mean1, variance4, residual mean.5 and zero variance residual,
KL=.5×.5²/4=.03125. Adding log-variance residual.2 gives.041951379nats.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hierarchy_hand.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n            = dm::nn;
  const auto          t  = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto          pm = n::full({1, 1}, 1, t), pl = n::full({1, 1}, std::log(4.), t);
  std::vector<double> residual, base, wider;
  for (int k = 0; k <= 20; ++k) {
    const double delta = k / 20.;
    residual.push_back(delta);
    for (double v : {0., .2}) {
      const auto q =
          n::models::HierarchyPosterior(pm, pl, n::full({1, 1}, delta, t), n::full({1, 1}, v, t));
      const double value = n::models::ConditionalGaussianKl(q.mu, q.log_variance, pm, pl).item();
      (v == 0 ? base : wider).push_back(value);
    }
  }
  if (base[0] != 0 || std::abs(base[10] - .03125) > 1e-14 ||
      std::abs(wider[10] - .5 * (std::exp(.2) - 1 - .2 + .25 / 4)) > 1e-14)
    return 1;
  auto chart = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  chart.lines(residual, base, "delta logvar0", dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .lines(residual, wider, "delta logvar.2", dm::RGBA{220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .title("Conditional KL / prior mean1, variance4")
      .x_label("residual mean correction")
      .y_label("KL / nats per coordinate")
      .save_svg("build/hierarchy_hand.svg");
  std::print(
      "Residual mean.5: KL {:.9f}; with logvar correction.2: {:.9f}.\n", base[10], wider[10]);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Residual mean.5: KL 0.031250000; with logvar correction.2: 0.041951379.
\image(figures/hierarchy_hand.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Zero residuals cost zero KL; mean corrections are weighted by the learned prior precision. A log-variance correction adds a separate variance-ratio cost.)
\alttext(Two convex curves show KL versus residual mean correction, with the positive log-variance correction lifting the curve.)

For inference, supplied epsilon has51 columns:49 lower-group draws first, then two top draws.
Changing lower epsilon changes only the lower sample and its decode; prior/posterior heads
stay fixed. Changing top epsilon changes z2, hence the learned prior **and** lower posterior.
The top KL is analytic to N(0,I); the lower conditional KL is analytic at each sampled z2.
The objective preserves the source grouping:

$$
L=\operatorname{mean}_x\left[\sum_{j=1}^{784}\{\operatorname{softplus}(a_j)-x_ja_j\}
+(K_1+K_2)\right],\qquad
K_2=\frac12\sum_{d=1}^2[mu_{2d}^2+e^{l_{2d}}-1-l_{2d}].
$$
\alttext(Pixel NLL,49-coordinate lower conditional KL and two-coordinate top KL are summed per image, then images are averaged.)

Four fixed validation draws average **every term** over draws and images, since the lower
KL depends on sampled top noise. Negative ELBO is a Monte Carlo bound estimate, not exact
marginal log likelihood. There are no free bits, variance clipping, KL annealing or flows.
Binary pixels give Bernoulli NLL; soft targets would give cross-entropy instead.

> The miniature NVAE cell and its separate moving state

Top projection maps two coordinates to196 values. The ordinary cell is a3×3 four-channel
Swish convolution. The NVAE cell instead expands4→8 with bias-free1×1 convolution, applies
BN/Swish, depthwise3×3 multiplier1, BN/Swish, projects8→4 with bias-free1×1 and BN, then
uses spatial-mean squeeze/excitation4→2Swish→4sigmoid. The result is input+.1(project×gate).
All weights are ordinary Glorot uniform; depthwise initialization is per input channel.
Biases are zero, BN gamma1/beta0. The cell has13 trainable tensors/198 scalars and six moving
buffers/40 scalars. Full counts are26 tensors/1977 scalars for the ordinary hierarchy and
37/2027 plus those buffers for miniature NVAE. Initialization order and seed2609 remain exact.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hierarchy_cell.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
