Ancestral generation uses the same20 explicit noise records for both variants: sample z2~N(0,I),
evaluate p(z1|z2), sample z1 using its own supplied epsilon, then decode both groups. There is
no image or posterior input. Top-noise interventions in the archive change learned prior and
posterior means; lower-noise interventions change only the sampled lower code. All inference,
prior and intervention calls are checked immutable in both parameter and moving-statistic stores.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hierarchy_priors.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::vector<n::Tensor> panels;
  for (const std::string model : {"hierarchical", "miniature_nvae"})
    for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz("build/hierarchy/" + model + "/prior.npz"))
      if (key == "probability")
        panels.push_back(n::reshape(value, {20, 28, 28, 1}));
  n::save_image_grid(n::concat(panels, 0), "build/hierarchy_priors.png", 5, 0, 1, 4, 220. / 255, 3);
}
