> Reproduction evidence and binding scope

The report compares all41 source NPZ archives by dtype, shape and every value byte, including
weights **and** moving buffers, first gradients/update, isolated prior-kernel gradients, all
epochs, selected arrays, nineteen fields for each greedy/four-draw pool evaluation, both noise
interventions and all ancestral endpoints. Batch/source order and full histories match exactly.
A separate optional NumPy oracle checks every first weight derivative, all six buffers in the first moving-statistic
update, Gaussian banks, posterior/prior fields, spectral gradients and six eligible criteria.
It performs no fitting, data preparation or downloads.

```
python3 scripts/check_hierarchical_variational_autoencoder.py docs/mepml/examples/nn/2_autoencoders/2.2_variational_autoencoders/build/hierarchy --json build/nn-report-conversion/hierarchical_vae/oracle.json
```

Native APIs are HierarchyPosterior, ConditionalGaussianKl, HierarchyNvaeCell, HierarchyDecode,
HierarchyPrior and HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder. Python and R expose the same operations;
tests retain Gaussian/ancestral/all19 forward fields after collection, exercise live Float64
inputs and separate caller-owned Adam/state updates, and verify image-free decoding. Native
checks include every small-model parameter derivative, BN/input/noise first/second derivatives,
default census, isolated two-kernel regularization and weight-plus-state restore. This does
not mark incomplete independent Python/R experiment loops as native ports. The ordinary
hierarchy and this miniature NVAE remain small two-group educational models.
